# Rank 1810 / CVE-2024-53947 (recorded CWE-89; the axis is CWE-184, an
# incomplete list of disallowed values): apache/superset executes SQL Lab and
# chart SQL behind a function-name denylist. BaseEngineSpec.execute
# (superset/db_engine_specs/base.py:1811) reads
# current_app.config["DISALLOWED_SQL_FUNCTIONS"], asks
# sql_parse.check_sql_functions_exist(query, disallowed, engine) whether the
# statement calls any named function, raises DisallowedSQLFunction when it
# does, and otherwise runs cursor.execute(query). The denylist
# (superset/config.py:1278) named four postgresql functions -- version,
# query_to_xml, inet_server_addr, inet_client_addr -- and omitted the rest of
# PostgreSQL's documented XML-producing family: query_to_xml_and_xmlschema,
# table_to_xml, table_to_xml_and_xmlschema, database_to_xml and their
# siblings all take a query/table/database (or serialize one opened)
# and return it as XML, so query_to_xml_and_xmlschema('select ...') executes
# the attacker's nested query text through a spelling the screen never
# consults. The fixed revision 0e002826 adds six strings to the set literal; it touches
# no code path. This is the second pass over the same list (CVE-2024-39887
# added the first four), which is the shape's own character: the project
# enumerates the family one advisory at a time.
#
# Measured, both revisions, three --cache off runs each over
# superset/config.py + superset/db_engine_specs + superset/sql_parse.py:
# 110 findings on the vulnerable parent and 110 on the fixed revision, identical sets;
# config.py's only findings are unrelated HardcodedSecret rows at lines
# 1177/1501/1668/1674/1683. The consumer's cursor.execute(query) is labelled
# code.SqlExecution (base.py:1831) and VYQL-INJ-001 does fire on a direct
# request -> execute repro, but on the real tree the request payload ->
# Query model -> execute_sql_statements -> spec.execute chain severs taint
# at the ORM persistence boundary (no SqlExecution finding on the sql_lab
# flow), and that path is identical in both revisions either way -- running
# user SQL is SQL Lab's purpose, and the guard call is present in both. The
# denylist has exactly one non-test consumer (grep over the tree), and the
# 1380 counterweight test fails: the fixed revision touches no guarded function, only
# the config set's contents.
#
# Reuse is checked before minting. The ontology's denylist-gap vocabulary
# (code.IncompleteDangerousUploadExtensionBlocklist,
# code.PickleUnsafeImportBlocklistGap,
# code.SandboxHostObjectMemberDenylistGap, code.IncompleteIpDenylist,
# code.JdbcSensitiveParamSinglePassFilterBypass) is the right class of
# judgement but each concept's vulnerableTo threat and reviewCondition name
# a different surface (upload extensions, pickle imports, sandbox members,
# IP ranges, JDBC connection parameters), so reuse would mis-file this
# finding. Minting a SQL-function-denylist sibling does not discriminate:
# a binding anchored on PostgreSQL's documentation (the whole XML-producing
# family) still fires on the patched revision, because the fixed revision itself adds
# only five of the family's documented spellings -- cursor_to_xml,
# cursor_to_xml_and_xmlschema, database_to_xml_and_xmlschema and the plain
# *_to_xmlschema variants are absent from the patched set too -- so the
# documentation-derived form flags both revisions and the only list that
# separates them is the fixing commit's own subset. That is the 1394 trap
# in data form. The corpus sweep is also empty: across the 430 clones under
# /tmp/cve-work/rank*/ no other file names any XML-family member or
# declares any SQL-function denylist (cross-checked pg_read_file/pg_sleep/
# dblink/lo_import; only a PHP builtins lexer table and jinja test
# fixtures), so the binding would fire on one product's one config file.
# ATTENTION, the configuration-content family (1514, 1535, 1540, 1556,
# 1557, 1593; missing-entry vocabulary like 1609/1614), not an engine gap:
# no construct is mishandled -- the distinguishing information is six
# strings in a set literal.
#
# All cases assert reject VYQL-INJ-001 (the taint rule consuming
# code.SqlExecution): nothing fires today because the query arrives as a
# parameter behind the ORM boundary and the denylist's contents carry no
# label. When SQL-function-denylist vocabulary lands, the first case must
# flip to expect while the patched, documentation-complete and
# no-consult-override cases stay reject.

test "rank1810: pg function denylist consult runs user SQL through a screen that names one family member"
  lang python
  reject VYQL-INJ-001
  file superset/config.py
  code
  ```
  DISALLOWED_SQL_FUNCTIONS: dict[str, set[str]] = {
      "postgresql": {"version", "query_to_xml", "inet_server_addr", "inet_client_addr"},
      "clickhouse": {"url"},
      "mysql": {"version"},
  }
  ```
  file superset/db_engine_specs/base.py
  code
  ```
  from flask import current_app

  from superset import sql_parse


  class DisallowedSQLFunction(Exception):
      def __init__(self, functions):
          super().__init__(f"disallowed SQL functions: {functions}")


  class BaseEngineSpec:
      engine = "postgresql"

      @classmethod
      def execute(cls, cursor, query, database, **kwargs):
          disallowed_functions = current_app.config["DISALLOWED_SQL_FUNCTIONS"].get(
              cls.engine, set()
          )
          if sql_parse.check_sql_functions_exist(query, disallowed_functions, cls.engine):
              raise DisallowedSQLFunction(disallowed_functions)

          cursor.execute(query)
  ```

